1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
What may you keep about a card?

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:06,912
When your bank calls you, they say: your card ending 7890.

3
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:09,336
They never ask for the three digits on the back.

4
00:00:09,386 --> 00:00:15,576
Now picture a support engineer who pastes a whole card number into a ticket, to check it with the bank.

5
00:00:15,626 --> 00:00:21,672
The number now lives in the ticket's backups, in its search index, and with everyone who reads tickets.

6
00:00:21,722 --> 00:00:25,056
One pasted number lives everywhere the ticket goes.

7
00:00:25,106 --> 00:00:28,152
Systems should talk about cards the way your bank does.

8
00:00:28,202 --> 00:00:32,472
The long number on the front of the card is the PAN, the card's own number.

9
00:00:32,522 --> 00:00:37,272
It lives in one place only, the card vault, and Lesson 4 shows why.

10
00:00:37,322 --> 00:00:40,656
The code on the back must never be kept once a payment is approved.

11
00:00:40,706 --> 00:00:42,960
And this system never even asks for it.

12
00:00:43,010 --> 00:00:49,848
Screens and logs may show the masked number: the first six digits and the last four, with nothing between.

13
00:00:49,898 --> 00:00:53,064
That is your bank's card ending 7890.

14
00:00:53,114 --> 00:00:58,080
The first digits only tell which bank issued the card, and Lesson 10 reads them.

15
00:00:58,130 --> 00:01:00,072
Other systems keep a card token.

16
00:01:00,122 --> 00:01:03,696
A card token is a random name the card vault gives the card.

17
00:01:03,746 --> 00:01:08,568
Nothing in it comes from the number, so the token tells a thief nothing about the card.

18
00:01:08,618 --> 00:01:11,928
In Lesson 4, the token becomes a coat-check ticket.

19
00:01:11,978 --> 00:01:13,800
Now back to the support ticket.

20
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:17,712
It should have held the masked number or the token, never the PAN.

21
00:01:17,762 --> 00:01:20,904
Now make both yourself, the way the code does.

22
00:01:20,954 --> 00:01:26,040
The card vault keeps two numbers: six digits shown first, and four shown last.

23
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:31,128
A token is a short prefix plus sixteen random bytes, written in hex.

24
00:01:31,178 --> 00:01:35,304
Open jshell, which comes with Java, and take a made-up card number.

25
00:01:35,354 --> 00:01:38,952
Keep the first six and the last four, and star out the rest.

26
00:01:39,002 --> 00:01:43,416
Then draw sixteen random bytes, and write them after the prefix.

27
00:01:43,466 --> 00:01:48,024
Run it again, and you get a different token, with nothing from the number in either.

28
00:01:48,074 --> 00:01:55,608
Your bank never asks for the code on the back, and our systems never hold the PAN: they hold the masked number and the token.

29
00:01:55,658 --> 00:01:58,056
Next: where a card number may live.
